                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "If your only criteria when judging a game is based on something as narrow as \"oh, well there's better games now so skip it\" then you are doing a disservice to the history of games development as a whole, but mostly a disservice to yourself since [b]you are bound to miss out on all of the thought-provoking classics that shaped the way for what we have now.[/b] For those of us who are truly fascinated by theories in game mechanics, games like [i]qrth-phyl[/i] are an absolute joy to play and revisit as well as [b]imperative to the learning process in understanding how gaming has arrived at the point that it currently is.[/b]\n\n[b]There isn't a single modern take on Snake as fleshed out or as interesting as qrth-phyl[/b], there may be more modern options like [i]Ms. Snake[/i] (which is a much messier 3D take on Snake) but they pale in comparison when you consider the time periods they were created in and the constraints developers had. For something made in 2012 before the big indie boom, [i]qrth-phyl[/i] is incomparable and leagues more ambitious for what it was doing than any similar games currently on Steam.\n\nIt's been a good 6 or so years since [i]qrth-phyl[/i]'s original release and there have been leaps and bounds in the evolution of arcade score-chasers here on Steam, and yet unsurprisingly with its rerelease [i]qrth-phyl[/i] still holds its own and [b]stands out as probably one of the more polished and atmospheric takes on the twisty-turny Snake formula[/b].\n\n[i]qrth-phyl[/i] was a beautiful shining diamond in the rough during the heyday of Xbox Live Indie's early and questionable days, a pure shot of score-chasing arcade adrenaline that couldn't be matched on the user generated and curated game service. \n\nEssentially, its [i]Snake[/i] in a 3D environment with a bit of a [i]Pac-Man[/i] collectathon that has you weaving inbetween your own trail to collect the bits needed for points, its simple but its smooth, it works, and soon dancing inbetween the polygonal trails is an elegant and seamless addiction. [b]The seamless transition from [i]Motos[/i]-like 2D playing grids to [i]Rez[/i]-like polygonal 3D worlds[/b] works perfectly and is still impressively executed, and all with the perfectly emulated sound effects of an old arcade cabinet. [b]Pure nostalgia bliss.[/b]\n\nHighly recommended for all of my arcade aficionado Steam pals, a classic and a [b]clear must-play for anyone who calls themselves an arcade enthusiast.[/b] If this weren't a remaster of something already released in the past, [b]this would be in my Top Games of 2018 right alongside [i]OK/NORMAL[/i], [i]Hyper Sentinel[/i] and [i]Floor Kids[/i].[/b]",
